## DATA ISSUES IDENTIFIED AND HOW TO HANDLE THEM

### Issue 1 — Exact duplicate rows in Usage_df
442,790 exact duplicate rows found (all 4 columns identical).
FIX: Remove with distinct()
Usage_df <- Usage_df %>% distinct()

### Issue 2 — Duplicate PANELISTIDs in User_df
85 unique panelists appear 4 times each instead of once.
Pattern: 2 distinct Weekday/Weekend values x 2 distinct Morning/Afternoon/Night 
values = 4 rows per panelist. Likely caused by two separate aggregation tables 
being joined without a time period key during data construction.
CLIENT GUIDANCE: Keep the second record (slice_tail) — differences are minimal 
and caused by database extraction artifacts.
FIX:
User_df <- User_df %>%
  group_by(PANELISTID) %>%
  slice_tail(n = 1) %>%
  ungroup()

### Issue 3 — Missing values in User_df (351 total NAs)
295 unique panelists have at least one NA. Missing values are almost entirely 
in AGE. The 57 panelists missing AGE are also missing GENDER (shown as Unknown)
and are 100% in the treated group with significantly higher usage than average.
These are NOT missing at random — they appear to be a distinct behavioral segment.
DECISION: Keep them as a separate segment. Do not drop. Flag with AGE_MISSING column.
User_df <- User_df %>% mutate(AGE_MISSING = is.na(AGE))

### Issue 4 — Unknown gender users (114 total)
57 have missing AGE, 57 have valid AGE but unknown gender.
All 114 are heavily skewed toward treated group (78% treated vs 10-21% for 
known genders). Higher usage across all time periods. Treat as distinct segment.

### Issue 5 — FOREGROUNDDURATION outliers
Values cluster near 20,000 minutes for some apps suggesting a data cap.
Values above 10,080 minutes per week are theoretically impossible.
Extreme values likely caused by background apps (Samsung Home UI, Settings) 
running passively while user does other things.
CLIENT GUIDANCE: Use IQR method or top 1%/5% percentile cutoffs. Do not use 
a fixed hard cutoff. Both treatment and control groups affected equally so 
directional bias is minimal.
FIX:
Q1 <- quantile(Usage_df$FOREGROUNDDURATION, 0.25, na.rm = TRUE)
Q3 <- quantile(Usage_df$FOREGROUNDDURATION, 0.75, na.rm = TRUE)
IQR_val <- Q3 - Q1
Usage_df <- Usage_df %>%
  mutate(IS_OUTLIER = FOREGROUNDDURATION > Q3 + 1.5 * IQR_val |
                      FOREGROUNDDURATION < Q1 - 1.5 * IQR_val)

### Issue 6 — Zero duration values
~14,000 observations with FOREGROUNDDURATION = 0. Mostly from one app (App Cache 
Walk). CLIENT GUIDANCE: Zeros are legitimate — users have apps installed they 
never open. Do not drop. Consider categorizing apps by usage frequency instead.

### Issue 7 — App name encoding issues
107,588 unique app names in APPDESCRIPTION. Many have garbled characters from 
encoding mismatches (non-Latin scripts, special characters showing as _x0003_ etc).
These are likely non-English apps. Cannot be classified by keyword matching.

